{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1087", "sample_idx": 3, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.1-8b-instruct", "content": "the social network is a 2010 american biographical drama film directed by david fincher and written by acid screenwriter jesse eisenberg, ron howard, and scott z. burns. the film is based on the book \"the accidental billionaires\" by ben mezrich, which tells the story of the founding of the social networking site facebook and the resulting lawsuits.\n\nthe film stars jesse eisenberg as mark zuckerberg, the co-founder and former CEO of facebook, and armie hammer as eric bosch, a fellow harvard student who claims to have been instrumental in the development of the site. the film also features joshua jackson as sean parker, a co-founder of napster and a key figure in the early days of facebook.\n\nthe social network received widespread critical acclaim for its direction, acting, and screenplay. the film was nominated for eight academy awards, including best picture, best director, and best original screenplay. it won three oscars, including best original score, best film editing, and best adapted screenplay.\n\none of the standout aspects of the social network is its portrayal of the founding of facebook and the subsequent lawsuits that ensued. the film takes a somewhat critical view of mark zuckerberg and the early days of facebook, highlighting the site's early exclusivity and the ways in which it was used to exclude and bully certain groups of people.\n\nthe film's portrayal of the early days of facebook is also notable for its attention to historical detail. the film's production team consulted with many of the key figures involved in the founding of facebook, including mark zuckerberg and eric bosch, to ensure that the film was as accurate as possible.\n\nthe social network also explores themes of class and privilege, as the film's characters are all members of the wealthy and well-connected elite. the film's portrayal of this world is often scathin